Historical evidence recorded before the skeleton migration. Counts, pending statuses, temporary handles and next-step instructions describe that checkpoint; consult the current development Record before acting.

The first physics block is installed in the repository. Nine new lessons cover system/question, coordinates/time, average and local motion, constant acceleration, momentum, work/energy/power, model evidence, a piecewise cart investigation and review. The sixteen-Record progression begins at `@physics-core` and ends at `@physics-foundation-review`. It reuses `ql-units`, `ql-graphs`, `ql-geometry`, `ql-trigonometry`, `ql-vectors` and `mech-force`, preserving their identities, imports, original prose and earlier learning routes. Geometry precedes the graph-area derivation. Logarithmic-axis work, general three-dimensional equilibrium and unfamiliar calculus have explicit later preparation.

The block reuses the existing University Physics Volume 1 source Record. Selected publisher openings in sections 3.1, 3.4, 5.1, 5.3, 7.3, 9.2 and 9.3, and the current web-edition notice, were inspected. Engineering Statics chapter 2/3 contents and its two-dimensional coordinate section were checked to bound the shared force lesson's preparation. The source note separates inspected passages from broader assigned reading. No whole-book audit or physical experiment is claimed, and no source media is stored locally.

The cart investigation connects independent motion, momentum and energy accounts. Its pulling and stopping intervals use separate models; the signed resistance model ends at rest. Finite observations do not uniquely determine a physical law, and internal-force cancellation in a total-momentum account does not erase relative motion, deformation or energy changes. Independent exact arithmetic passed 212 comparisons, including graph/rate calculations, reversal, impulse, work and power, and stepwise force integration with varied masses, forces, initial velocities and subdivisions.

Installation followed a concurrent-edit comparison and a preservation audit across all sixteen completed Hephaestus subject compositions. Their discovery sets are unchanged and none imports the broad Hephaestus/physics map. This evidence supersedes the earlier temporary instruction to freeze installation until the entire regression run finished: the remaining tests exercise unchanged subject imports and routes, while export uses already loaded text. The original run continues; it is not a clean rerun of the new broad-core test. The separate physics snapshot test covers the new broad composition and remains pending.

